WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.380
The first film said force follows the balance of kinase and phosphatase.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.840
That holds while force is being built.

3
00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:11.200
Keep a strip of artery muscle stimulated.

4
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:16.040
Phosphorylation rises, then falls back toward low values.

5
00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:18.820
Yet force stays near its peak.

6
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.180
What is holding the load?

7
00:00:24.633 --> 00:00:28.333
If force simply tracked phosphorylation, it would follow it down.

8
00:00:29.713 --> 00:00:33.233
In 1988, Hai and Murphy proposed a kinetic model.

9
00:00:34.573 --> 00:00:39.393
Each myosin head is phosphorylated or not, and attached or not: four states.

10
00:00:40.813 --> 00:00:44.173
Kinase and phosphatase act on heads whether or not they are bound.

11
00:00:45.283 --> 00:00:48.343
So the balance still sets how many heads carry a phosphate.

12
00:00:49.623 --> 00:00:53.923
But a head that loses its phosphate while attached does not release at once.

13
00:00:55.103 --> 00:00:59.423
It becomes a latch bridge, and its slow detachment is the rate-limiting step.

14
00:01:00.483 --> 00:01:01.963
Here is the one idea.

15
00:01:03.243 --> 00:01:07.483
The phosphate switches heads on; attached heads that lose it let go slowly.

16
00:01:08.763 --> 00:01:13.243
So force can outlast the phosphate, while cycling and its ATP cost fall.

17
00:01:14.283 --> 00:01:16.503
Take one illustrative run of the model.

18
00:01:17.623 --> 00:01:25.363
A steady 17 percent phosphorylation held about 86 percent of the force held at 67

19
00:01:26.043 --> 00:01:27.783
Most of it sat on latch bridges.

20
00:01:28.923 --> 00:01:31.143
The figure depends on the constants chosen.

21
00:01:32.463 --> 00:01:36.963
The slow release is a model assumption, not a structure anyone has imaged.

22
00:01:37.967 --> 00:01:40.127
Latch is not a state without ATP.

23
00:01:41.787 --> 00:01:45.847
Phosphorylated cross-bridges keep cycling, and latch bridges let go slowly.

24
00:01:47.147 --> 00:01:50.427
Nor is it rigor, the stiff state of a muscle out of ATP.

25
00:01:51.987 --> 00:01:53.547
Latch is economy, not shutdown.

26
00:01:54.717 --> 00:02:01.357
In 2022, Hammell and colleagues tested this on a few purified myosin molecules in a

27
00:02:01.357 --> 00:02:06.657
laser trap. Once force reached a plateau, they injected the phosphatase.

28
00:02:08.117 --> 00:02:12.817
Tonic myosin, from pig stomach, held force for a median of 32 seconds.

29
00:02:14.217 --> 00:02:17.157
Phasic myosin, from chicken gizzard, held for 21.

30
00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:21.160
But the phosphates were not timed on the trapped heads.

31
00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:27.460
A parallel assay put the midpoint of dephosphorylation near 27 seconds.

32
00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:32.440
Which hold is the stronger evidence that force outlasted the phosphate?

33
00:02:38.533 --> 00:02:44.273
The tonic hold. Only it outlasted that clock, so it is the more convincing.

34
00:02:45.653 --> 00:02:48.053
The phasic hold ended before the midpoint.

35
00:02:49.393 --> 00:02:53.553
And these were purified pig and chicken proteins, not an intact vessel.

36
00:02:54.617 --> 00:02:58.437
So once force is built, force and phosphorylation part ways.

37
00:02:59.777 --> 00:03:01.737
The balance sets how many heads switch on.

38
00:03:03.137 --> 00:03:05.697
Slow release decides how long the load stays up.

39
00:03:06.757 --> 00:03:10.937
That is how smooth muscle, above all tonic muscle, holds force cheaply.
